

No! Not this! Of all the stupid, dangerous, unfair, preposterously unlikely
accidents... And why did it have to happen today, on your first climb together?

Easy now, stay calm. Don't panic. It's just that it's all your fault! Well, no
it isn't, but Judith trusted you with her life, and you screwed up.

And now she will hate you for what you are about to do. But you have to do it.
It's the only way to save both your lives. She must understand.
[32]

> look
By the crevice
A misplaced step, a slip. The rope is pinning your arm down.

> x rope
The rope stretches across your right arm, over the edge of the crevice. Several
metres below, Judith dangles from its other end.

A snowflake drifts through the cold air.

> x judith
Judith is in the crevice, dangling off the far end of your rope, a short
distance from the ground. She manages to look frightened and annoyed at the same
time.

> x me
You are Charles Wong, 29 years old, aspiring writer and gentleman mountaineer.

Snow is falling.

> x harness
It's a mountaineering harness, securely fastened to your body with a large loop
around the waist and two smaller loops for the legs. Attached to the harness is
a tool belt, and a rope.

> x tool belt
Hanging from the tool belt is your trusty pocket knife.

> take knife
You take the pocket knife off the tool belt.

The snowfall is increasing.

> cut rope with knife
You brace yourself, send a solemn, confident look in the general direction of
Judith, and hope that she'll understand. She probably can't see you anyway;
you're having a hard time making out her contours beyond the whirling snow.
Anyway, you know this mountain, and she'll only have a short distance to fall
before hitting the soft snow below.

The rope doesn't break as easily as you'd imagined. For what feels like an
eternity, you saw through it one strand at a time, until it finally gives.

Endorphins rush through your system, rewarding you for alleviating the pain in
your right arm which is no longer pinned down. But this is no time to rest, and
the weather conditions keep getting worse. You begin the arduous climb back down
in order to get help.
[32]

It is taking longer than you expected. As the sun begins to set, the snowstorm
intensifies. Soon everything is darkness.
[32]

