> sleep
(first attempting to get onto your bed)
You lie down on the bed.

It's really cold now. You're shaking.

You toss and turn, but it's too hot to sleep. You're going to have to get out of
those pyjamas.

> remove pyjamas
You take off your flannel pyjamas.

> sleep
You close your eyes and allow a wave of tiredness to wash over you.

Like every night for the past thirty years, your thoughts return to Judith, and
to that gruesome day in the mountains. When you returned after four hours with
the mountain rescue volunteers, she was suffering from severe hypothermia. She
had stumbled over the Swallet cave system, and sought shelter inside. Knowing
her, you assume she started to explore them, exhausting herself in the process.
As the night went on, her body temperature would have dropped gradually, and she
would have started to behave erratically. When you found her, she was
unconscious, in a state of undress, burrowed in a corner near the entrance. She
died later that night in the hospital.

At first you were crushed by acute feelings of pain and guilt. But through the
years you've developed a more balanced attitude towards your grieving, and even
begun to reenact and explore variations of the incident, here in the privacy of
your apartment. If she knew about your solitary role playing, she'd roll her
eyes. But it's how you keep her memory alive.

You were told to cultivate new friendships, to learn to trust other people and
to share your plight with them. It would help you to move on, they said. But you
are inescapably entangled with Judith's memory, with strings that can never be
severed. And you know that if you were to seek the company of others, you would
be diluting that connection to the point where she ceases to be a unique
snowflake, and is lost forever.

As you drift away into sleep, you smile wistfully at a stray thought: The
strongest bond you ever made was the result of cutting a rope.

